						    <h1 class="page-title ">
        Measure M    </h1>

							
							
<div class="entry-content">
	<p style="text-align: center;"><span style="font-size: 12pt;"><strong>MEASURE M</strong></span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;"><strong>CITY OF CLAYTON</strong></span></p>
<p style="text-align: left;"><span style="font-size: 12pt;">CITY OF CLAYTON SALES TAX MEASURE.</span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">To provide local funding to maintain City of Clayton programs, services, and facilities, including: police services, traffic enforcement and road safety, road and sidewalk improvements, enhance library and recreation opportunities, park maintenance, recruitment and retention of city employees and other city services, shall the City of Clayton levy a 1% sales tax until ended by voters generating approximately $1,000,000 annually, subject to public review, the annual audit and all funds benefitting Clayton?</span></p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><span style="font-size: 12pt;"><strong>CITY ATTORNEY’S IMPARTIAL ANALYSIS OF<br />
MEASURE M</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">On June 16, 2026, the City Council voted unanimously to place Measure M on the November 3, 2026 General Municipal Election ballot.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">If approved by a majority of Clayton voters, this Measure would authorize a one percent (1%) retail transactions and use (sales) tax within the City of Clayton. A one percent (1%) rate equates to an extra $1 per $100 purchased. It is estimated that Measure M will provide an additional $1,000,000 in annual local funding that could be used for City of Clayton programs, services and facilities, including police services, traffic enforcement and road safety programs, road and sidewalk improvements, enhancing library and recreation services, park maintenance, and recruitment and retention of City employees. This tax would be a “general tax,” meaning that revenues raised from the tax would go into the City’s general fund to pay for any lawful City program, improvement, or service.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">The California Constitution (Proposition 218) and Revenue and Taxation Code section 7285.9 authorize the City to levy a general transactions and use/sales tax at a rate of one percent (1%) so long as the tax is approved by a majority of the voters voting in an election on that issue. If approved, the tax would become operative on April 1, 2027 and remain in effect until repealed by voters at a subsequent election.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">The tax would be paid in addition to current sales taxes and would be collected at the same time and in the same manner as existing sales taxes. All revenues raised by the tax would remain in the City and would not be shared with the State, County or any other agency.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">A “yes” vote on Measure M will authorize the 1% transactions and use (sales) tax.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">A “no” vote on Measure M will not authorize the 1% transactions and use (sales) tax.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;"><strong>The above statement is an impartial analysis of Measure M. If you desire a copy of the Measure, please call the Office of the City Clerk at (925) 673-7300 and a copy will be mailed at no cost to you.</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">/s/ Malathy Subramanian</span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">City Attorney</span></p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><span style="font-size: 12pt;"><strong>ARGUMENT IN FAVOR OF</strong></span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;"><strong>MEASURE M</strong></span></p>
<p><strong><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Measure M is an investment in Clayton’s quality of life designed to protect our city’s future.</span></strong></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Clayton will soon be facing a long-term structural deficit of $1.18 million due to rising costs of delivering essential services.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">This deficit means that without action, the City will struggle to sustain our current police services, repair our streets and sidewalks, preserve parks and recreation programs, and maintain aging infrastructure.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Measure M offers a long-term solution to our deficit through a 1% sales tax increase. Projections estimate this will generate $1 million annually. This tax increase will ensure the City has the resources to continue delivering the quality services residents depend on.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Since Clayton’s founding, City leaders have always fought to avoid tax increases.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Before proposing Measure M, the City exhausted every other available means of generating new revenue. This included updating fees, reorganizing staff, and reducing service contracts. Those measures have helped, but are simply not enough to close our deficit.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">The City does not propose this lightly. Measure M would be our first sales tax increase in the City’s sixty-two year history, which is why Clayton’s sales tax rate is among the lowest in the County.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Measure M is a local decision designed to meet local priorities. Should residents choose to approve Measure M, all of the resulting funds would remain accountable to residents and under the control of the City, not the County or State. That means 100% of all revenues generated stay in Clayton.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Clayton is a special community defined by safe neighborhoods, well-maintained parks, and a high quality of life.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">By acting now, we can protect the services that make Clayton a desirable place to live, work, and raise a family while avoiding more difficult and expensive choices later.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Kim Trupiano, Councilmember</span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">Holly Tillman, Councilmember</span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">Jeff Wan, Mayor</span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">Richard G. Enea, Vice-Mayor</span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">Jim Diaz, Councilmember</span></p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong><span style="font-size: 12pt;">REBUTTAL TO ARGUMENT IN FAVOR OF</span></strong><br />
<strong><span style="font-size: 12pt;">MEASURE M</span></strong></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">The City of Clayton’s government-requested 1% sales-tax hike shows indifference to the unique challenges our citizens face in their daily lives. In addition, the tax measure lacks transparency and does not address key infrastructure deficiencies.</span></p>
<p><strong><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Cost of Living Insensitivity</span></strong><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">● </span><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Homeowner insurance policies are rising steeply due to Clayton’s high fire-risk rating.</span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">● </span><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Contractor costs for maintenance and mitigating fire risks around our homes drain family savings.</span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">● </span><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Childcare and college costs are at record highs, crushing working families already hit by high housing expenses.</span></p>
<p><strong><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Flawed Financial Benchmarking and Deficits</span></strong><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">● </span><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Pegging Clayton’s small-town employee salaries to other city governments’ rates ensures that future spending will continue upward, likely requiring more tax increases.</span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">● </span><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Clayton’s government has not identified total long-term funding needs for deferred infrastructure maintenance, such as for streets and sidewalks, other liabilities, and capital replacement needs.</span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">● </span><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Raising taxes to fund staff salary increases — without an action plan to fix crumbling infrastructure — makes no fiscal sense and likely leads to future tax hikes.</span></p>
<p><strong><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Transparency and Tax Fatigue</span></strong><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">● </span><span style="font-size: 12pt;">The City lacked recent, thorough tax-measure development engagement with working families, business owners, and professional groups.</span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">● </span><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Voters face mounting financial burdens: around 35% of Clayton households saw significant increases in Geological Hazard Abatement District (GHAD) property tax fees last year, and the city’s Landscape Maintenance District tax is up for renewal; yet a forever 1% sales tax increase is demanded.</span></p>
<p><strong><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Please vote No on Measure M, as it aggressively increases taxes on tangible purchases like cars, clothes, and electronics.</span></strong></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Contra Costa Taxpayers Association, Marc D. Joffe, President</span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">James Killoran, Credit Union Supervisory Committee Chair, Clayton</span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">Allison Snow, Retired Healthcare Executive, Clayton</span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">Roy Ashworth, Retired Financial Analyst, Clayton</span></p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong><span style="font-size: 12pt;">ARGUMENT AGAINST</span></strong><br />
<strong><span style="font-size: 12pt;">MEASURE M</span></strong></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;"><strong>Vote No on the 1% Clayton Sales Tax.</strong> We sent a message in June. Now, let’s stand firm in November.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">• </span><span style="font-size: 12pt;">In June 2026, Clayton residents joined voters across Contra Costa County to decisively defeat Measure B, a 0.625% sales tax hike. <strong>In Clayton, 69% of voters wisely said No to Measure B.</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">• </span><span style="font-size: 12pt;">The message from our community was clear: inflation and the cost of living are hurting families, and we cannot afford more taxes. Yet, just weeks later, the Clayton City Council voted 5-0 to push an even larger 1% tax increase onto the November ballot.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">• </span><span style="font-size: 12pt;">This proposal<strong> ignores the clear financial mandate</strong> Clayton citizens just delivered at the polls.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">• </span><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Clayton’s overall sales tax rate is an already high 8.75%; <strong>Measure M would spike that rate to 9.75%</strong>, punishing working families and fixed-income seniors.<strong> If Measure RTM also passes, our total rate would reach 10.25%.</strong></span></p>
<p><strong><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Fuel Staff Pay Hikes:</span></strong></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">• </span><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Our politicians have <strong>no set agreement</strong> for how the proposed tax hike will be used.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">• </span><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Ultimately, this revenue increase to the general fund will likely be absorbed by rising city salaries, continuing to leave deferred infrastructure neglected and triggering future tax hikes to pay for actual community needs.</span></p>
<p><strong><span style="font-size: 12pt;">A Losing Compensation Race:</span></strong></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">• </span><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Government salaries and employee benefits have risen significantly since the pandemic, while the private sector faces layoffs. Between 2019 and 2025, total Clayton employee compensation rose 37%, well ahead of Bay Area consumer price inflation.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">• </span><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Setting Clayton’s small-town employee salaries to other city governments ensures that future spending will likely exceed revenues, requiring more tax increases.</span></p>
<p><strong><span style="font-size: 12pt;">No End in Sight:</span></strong></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">• </span><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Unlike the failed Measure B, which was set to expire after 5 years, Clayton’s Measure M is a <strong>permanent</strong> tax hike.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">• </span><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Clayton government must learn to live within its means, just like Clayton families do every single day.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Keep Clayton Sensible. <strong>Vote No on Measure M.</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Contra Costa Taxpayers Association, Marc D. Joffe, President</span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">James Killoran, Credit Union Supervisory Committee Chair, Clayton</span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">Allison Snow, Retired Healthcare Executive, Clayton</span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">Nancy Topp, CPA/CFO, Clayton</span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">Bill Calkins, Clayton Resident</span></p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong><span style="font-size: 12pt;">REBUTTAL TO ARGUMENT AGAINST</span></strong><br />
<strong><span style="font-size: 12pt;">MEASURE M</span></strong></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;"><strong>Measure M is about Clayton’s future and the city’s ability to maintain the services residents depend on every day.</strong> Clayton faces a projected $1.18 million structural deficit despite efforts to improve efficiency through fee updates, staffing reorganization, contract reductions, and other cost-saving measures. Those steps helped, but they did not eliminate the ongoing funding gap.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Supporters view Measure M’s 1% sales tax increase as a practical investment in maintaining public safety, repairing streets and sidewalks, preserving parks and public facilities, and sustaining essential city services. Clayton’s sales tax rate remains lower than that of many neighboring communities, and stable local revenue would help the city retain qualified employees, avoid costly turnover, and plan responsibly for the future.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Opponents argue the measure does not directly solve deferred maintenance. However, without additional recurring revenue, deferred maintenance will continue to grow, leaving fewer resources for streets, landscaping, parks, and city facilities. Once services are reduced or positions are eliminated, restoring them later can be difficult and expensive.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;"><strong>Measure M</strong> provides locally controlled funding that stays in Clayton and supports the core services residents rely on. Rather than relying on temporary fixes, it offers a long-term approach to addressing the city’s financial challenges and protecting the quality of life that makes Clayton a desirable place to live. Voting <strong>Yes on Measure M</strong> is a vote for a safe, well-maintained, and financially sustainable community.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-size: 12pt;">Kim Trupiano, Councilmember</span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">Holly Tillman, Councilmember</span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">Richard G. Enea, Vice-Mayor</span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">Jeff Wan, Mayor</span><br />
<span style="font-size: 12pt;">Jim Diaz, Council Member</span></p>
</div>

